Figure 3 illustrates that Nano-11 induces a transient inflammatory response at the injection site in mice, characterized by the recruitment of various immune cells over time. Panel a shows the kinetics of inflammatory cell infiltration, with Mac-2+ macrophages and antigen-presenting cells peaking around day 2, while neutrophils are abundant early but decline quickly. Panel b presents confocal microscopy images, demonstrating that Alexa Fluor 647-labeled Nano-11 (red) is taken up intracellularly by MHCII+ (APCs), Ly6C+ (monocytes), and Mac-2+ (macrophages) cells at the injection site over 7 days. This highlights Nano-11's role in local immune activation and efficient targeting of antigens to key antigen-presenting cells.